{{also|Goon|góon|gōon|go on|go-on|ĝo-on}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=simpleton|from|gooney>albatross|tree=1}}
Shortened from {{m|en|gooney}}, from obsolete {{m|en||gony|t=simpleton}}, used circa 1580, of {{unk|en|title=unknown}} origin. Perhaps a familiar term derived from {{der|en|enm|gone}}, a variant of {{m|enm|gome|t=man, person}}. ''Gony'' was applied by sailors to the [[albatross]] and similar big, clumsy birds (circa 1839). The term ''goon'' first carried the meaning "stupid person" (circa 1921). Compare {{cog|sco|goni}}, {{m|sco|guni|t=a bogey, bugbear, hobgoblin}}, dialectal {{cog|sv|gonnar|t=elves, goblins|pos=plural}}.

* {{senseno|en|thug|uc=1}} ("hired thug"; circa 1938) is largely influenced by the comic strip character {{w|Alice the Goon}} from the ''{{w|Popeye}}'' series.
* {{senseno|en|fool|uc=1}} ("fool") was reinforced by the popular radio program, ''{{w|The Goon Show}}'', starring {{w|Spike Milligan}} and {{w|Peter Sellers}}.
* {{senseno|en|German guard|uc=1}} ("guard") was influenced by both {{senseno|en|thug}} and {{senseno|en|fool}}, though not by ''The Goon Show'' reference, which arose about 10 years after WWII.

